<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emerald Mound</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1250</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emerald Mound is a large Mississippian-period platform mound near Natchez, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, built from about 1250 by ancestors of the Natchez people. Covering about 3 hectares, it is one of the largest such mounds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, an earthen platform topped by smaller mounds for temples or elite dwellings. It was largely abandoned by the late 17th century, when the Natchez centre had moved to the Grand Village.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emerald-mound.jpg" alt="Emerald Mound" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elisa Rolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emerald_Mound_Site.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Emerald Mound, also known as the Selsertown Site, lies on the Natchez Trace Parkway near Stanton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. It belongs to the Plaquemine culture of the Mississippian period and is the type site of the Emerald Phase, 1500 to 1680, in the archaeological chronology of the Natchez Bluffs. The platform mound is the second-largest Mississippian earthwork in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, after Monk's Mound at Cahokia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>.</p><p>Builders reshaped a natural hill by piling earth along its sides into an elongated, five-sided plateau 770 feet (230 m) by 435 feet (133 m) at the base and 35 feet (11 m) high, its summit about 65 feet (20 m) above the surrounding land. Two secondary mounds stand at either end of the summit; the larger, at the western end, is 30 feet (9.1 m) high. Six smaller mounds once flanked the edges and a ditch encircled the complex, but plowing and erosion in the 19th century flattened the mounds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Emerald was the ceremonial centre for a population living in outlying villages and hamlets. Its rituals took place on the top of the great mound, a feature rarely seen at other mound centres, and the secondary mounds carried a temple and the residence of a priest or ruler. Archaeologists believe its Plaquemine builders were ancestors of the Natchez, who still used it as their main ceremonial centre at the time of first European contact. Its decline may have followed the social upheaval caused by European diseases introduced by the de Soto expedition in the 1540s, and by the La Salle Expedition of 1682 the main centre was at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-village-of-the-natchez">Grand Village of the Natchez</a>, 12 miles (19 km) to the south-west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The first recorded excavations took place in 1838, when investigators measured the mound, exhumed pottery and skeletons and noted the secondary mounds and an encircling trench; the most recent was in 1972. The owners donated the site to the National Park Service in the 1950s, which restored and stabilised the mounds. It was declared a National Historic Landmark in 1989 and is open to the public as part of the Natchez Trace Parkway. Its name comes from the Emerald Plantation that surrounded it in the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emerald Mound site” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monks Mound at Cahokia, about 30 metres high and some 6 hectares at its base, is the largest prehistoric earthwork in the Americas; Mound A at Etowah rises 19 metres, and <b>Emerald Mound</b> near Natchez, Mississippi, covers about 3 hectares.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Emerald Mound</b>, one of the largest platform mounds in North America, was built from around 1250, and by the late 17th century the political centre was the Grand Village on St Catherine Creek, near the modern city of Natchez, Mississippi.</blockquote><a href="/state/natchez-chiefdom">Natchez chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
